course: dictionary
lesson: 370
title: Antipathy: a strong dislike
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Antipathy is a formal word for a strong feeling of dislike, the kind you have towards something you cannot stand. You can say antipathy towards someone or something, or antipathy to it.
explain: Alice mixes up two long words. «Alice mixes up antipodes (the people on the opposite side of the Earth) with antipathies (strong dislikes), a funny word mistake called a malapropism.» Look at the clues: she is falling down a hole and thinks about people walking upside down, which fits antipodes, not dislikes.
explain: To check a word, swap your meaning into the sentence. «She wondered if she would fall right through the earth and come out among people walking upside down» makes sense with antipodes. Put in dislikes and it does not, so the word you need is antipodes.
fact: Antipathy means a strong feeling of dislike.
fact: Antipathy can also mean something that a person strongly dislikes.
fact: Antipathy is a formal word, so you will meet it more in writing than in chat.
fact: Antipathy is often followed by «towards» or «to», as in antipathy towards cold, wet weather.
fact: «Antipodes» means the people on the opposite side of the Earth, so do not mix it up with antipathy.
exercise: Alice wonders about «the Antipathies». What does antipathy mean? | *A strong feeling of dislike | The people on the opposite side of the Earth | A long fall down a rabbit hole
exercise: Alice means the Antipodes, not the Antipathies. What are the Antipodes? | Very long words | *The people on the opposite side of the Earth | Strong dislikes | A deep rabbit hole
exercise: Ben feels a strong ___ towards broccoli. Which word fits best? | Antipodes | Antidote | Antipasto | *Antipathy
test: Which small word usually follows antipathy? | *towards | about | with | at
test: What is the best meaning of antipathy? | Someone who falls very far | A type of rabbit hole | Something that is loved very much | *Something that is strongly disliked
exam: Why is Alice's mix-up of antipodes and antipathies funny? | *The two long words sound alike but mean very different things | Carroll never wrote about the Earth | Both words are about rabbits | Alice is wrong about falling
